@endpoint = http://localhost:3000/api/v1

### W1 Register – expect 201, email lowercased, no passwordHash
POST {{endpoint}}/auth/register HTTP/1.1
content-type: application/json

{
    "name": "Adri",
    "email": "Adri@Example.com",
    "password": "difficult password"
}

### W2 Register bad email – expect 400
POST {{endpoint}}/auth/register HTTP/1.1
content-type: application/json

{
    "name": "Adri",
    "email": "not-an-email",
    "password": "difficult password"
}

### W3 Register short password – expect 400
POST {{endpoint}}/auth/register HTTP/1.1
content-type: application/json

{
    "name": "Adri",
    "email": "adri3@example.com",
    "password": "123"
}

### W4 Login – expect 200 { token }   (run W1 first)
# @name login
POST {{endpoint}}/auth/login HTTP/1.1
content-type: application/json

{
    "email": "adri@example.com",
    "password": "difficult password"
}

### W5 Wrong password – expect 401 "Invalid email or password"
POST {{endpoint}}/auth/login HTTP/1.1
content-type: application/json

{
    "email": "adri@example.com",
    "password": "wrong password"
}

### W6 Unknown user – expect 401 with the SAME message
POST {{endpoint}}/auth/login HTTP/1.1
content-type: application/json

{
    "email": "nobody@example.com",
    "password": "difficult password"
}

###
@accessToken = {{login.response.body.token}}

### W7 Me with token – expect 200 + your user   (run W4 first)
GET {{endpoint}}/auth/me HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

### W8 Me without token – expect 401 "Missing or invalid Authorization header"
GET {{endpoint}}/auth/me HTTP/1.1

### W9 Delete without token – expect 401
DELETE {{endpoint}}/cars/REAL_ID_HERE HTTP/1.1

### W10 Delete with token but bad id – expect 400 (auth runs before validation)
DELETE {{endpoint}}/cars/123 HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

### W11 Delete with token – expect 200
DELETE {{endpoint}}/cars/REAL_ID_HERE HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}